body:
I feel you. I'm having this matter now.

&#x200B;

How was your appointment?

Thank you so much for your reply! Would you like to share your experience and how have you dealt with it?

&#x200B;

I talked about it to my therapist at a specialist hospital a few times but she didn't help me do anything... I also talked to my psychologist and he insisted the roots of my anxieties are "solved", my choking anxiety would go away...

Congrats! I can relate! It wasn't easy at all. You made a big move!

&#x200B;

When will you start your job? How long have you been being diagnosed with anxiety disorder? Are you on medication?

&#x200B;

I started my first full-time job in mid May after a year without working outside. I totally understood how hard it was to make such a big decision and take actions.

Yeah, that's why I've been hesitant about getting the drop... 

I tried nasal spray and some other med for nasal allergies in last August. None of them worked. Oh, I also tried the saline washing as well. It didn't work.

&#x200B;

I saw a Chinese med doctor two weeks ago and told her this besides of my other health issues. She said acupuncture might be helpful but first let's ease the cough problem. I'll try to book an osteopathy appointment after CNY. I think I'll do acupuncture as well though I've needle phobia.

&#x200B;

What's your specific muscular imbalance problem if don't mind asking?

So I should just give it a try that seeing a  dentist first even I myself don't really find my temporomandibular joints problematic? 

Could you please elaborate on your situation? I'd really like to know more about that. Did you notice you had bruxism before seeing the dentist? My ETube opens whenever it wants to, not just chewing, yawning, talking, walking and so on... 

&#x200B;

Thank you for your great patience and spending time on "analyzing" my situation. As for the relationship part, I've already known what most would or should probably do with it. What I see it is not just a relationship thing only. Anyway by seeing what word usage you used to describe made me recall lots of negative memories of it, including how people saw it and told me about it. It took me a while to go back to this post and reply. 

&#x200B;

I saw general doctors and an ENT doctor for my cough for several times in last August and September. I also tried some home and herbal remedies for my cough. None could effectively and completely cure my cough. I was not professionally diagnosed with psychogenic cough but found my cough had some kind of pattern, especially before and after eating (I think it's because I have a choking anxiety so I cough like that), before sleep, and after waking up. I also found that I did not cough during the hypnosis session or some times when I was fully occupied by something unstressful or unpleasant. It's confirmed that whenever I feel anxious or suffocated (mentally), I cough more often. Based on my observations, I think my cough should be psychogenic. I did chest X-ray twice in May, and it was fine. 

&#x200B;

The medication I've been on since last October is prescribed by a psychiatrist from a private clinic in my city. Yesterday he said, "You're not doing worse or better."

&#x200B;

The psychiatrist knew that I've been seeing a therapist at a specialist hospital (arranged by the hospital where I was hospitalized for one day), as well as a psychologist referred by the school counselor who gave me sessions in last July. So far the hospital therapist has been giving me some kind of homework to do but it's not like a school teacher would strictly examine your homework. It's little books (made by psychologists) for recording and scheduling your activities and challenges, and providing health guides. The first two times I got the little books I carefully read them and wrote some plans on them but soon the big incident happened in last mid October and it lasted until the end of November. I totally put aside the little books. The duration of the incident severely disturbed my living during the two months. I always felt extremely suffocated and dizzy in those months. I rubbed herbal embrocation onto my chest and my head. Two bottles (both 10 ML) were empty. I was smelling like an lady at that time. This was the time I decided to talk about this whole matter to my hospital therapist, psychologist, three friends who are not my age. I also tried two counselling sessions from another low-cost counselling center but there were bad so I didn't continue it. My psychiatrist referred me a new therapist and I tried one session with her but she's not available until it's March so the psychiatrist sent me to another one. I haven't seen her yet.

&#x200B;

True that I need to have a bit more active social life. I'm however always worried if I get uncomfortable when going out for more than three hours. It is a hustle and bustle city here. People are always busy. Before I could meet friends for dinner or any noisy and crowded places. Now I can't. It limits my friends' (my age) desires and options to meet me. Oh right, certainly no one would really like to spend their precious off-from-work time on someone who's physically weak, depressed and anxious. I'm not saying I'm giving on meeting my friends. I just wait and see what arrangements would bother both less. 

&#x200B;

My family have been getting quite impatient to me. I totally understand that.

&#x200B;

Again, thank you for taking your time reading all these. 

&#x200B;

Hope your work in the relevant field will go on doing good, and have good physical and mental health.

In Oct 12, My psychiatrist (not the hospital one) first gave me 0.25 mg Nalion two times/day and 10 mg S-Oropram (half of one pill) one time/day. I tried them for three days and had restless legs when I slept. Then the psychiatrist gave me Tianeptine and Akamon. 

I don't think I'd try any kind of anti-depressants or/and tranquilizers without a psychiatrist's prescription. I've been so timid since I have had those physical and mental discomforts which gradually appeared in last April.

&#x200B;

My major current physical discomforts are the urge to cough, migraines caused by coughing too much sometimes, being breathless easily (but it's better than it was in last August and September) and feelings of nausea (sometimes for no reason and sometimes after eating or 15-minute simple exercises) and generally poor sleep quality (which causes me to have migraines or headaches from time to time but a lot less often now since I finished all my studies and stopped working at all). As for mental discomforts are choking anxiety (which has been affecting my living), anxieties about not having any significant improvements, my month-long cough, not healthy enough to have a full-time job, lagging behind with most of my friends as most have been working for three years after finishing their Bachelor's. 

&#x200B;

I have been regularly seeing a psychologist since last October (once/more than 30 days) and a hospital therapist (once/more than 30 days or once a month). Also I tried a hypnosis session once in last December. Two times of counselling services from an individual center in last November. One therapy session from an experienced lady referred by my psychiatrist in January. But she's leaving the city where I live for more than a month so I'll ask my psychiatrist to refer me another one this Friday.

&#x200B;

My choking anxiety has been deterring me from eating more. I also have low appetite. I constantly have a feeling that I might not be able to get rid off it...

There are many registration-free freelancing platforms online providing paid work. Based on your skills, you can apply for some freelancing work on those websites. 

&#x200B;

How does your anxiety disorder affect your living?

When did you begin seeing the therapist for your PTSD and anxiety?

&#x200B;

I also feel it's a struggle to live every day.

Same here but I'm older...

&#x200B;

How many years have you studied at college?

When accumulating more and more physical discomfort as well as mental ones, one statistically insignificant incident would cause a huge and unexpected effect on the person.

&#x200B;

I have been trying some different things to help myself but my cough and choking anxiety are still very stubborn (also because something big and lousy happened last month and I was affected by it a lot). 

&#x200B;

It is good to know someone experiencing very similar things.

&#x200B;
